	Prepared by John Stafford, Winona State University Department of
	Academic Computing Services, in cooperation with the Academic
	Computing Committee.

 Once approved by the committee, this document is submitted
	to the Student Senate and Faculty Senate for approval or
	modification.

	A final, approved version is then published.  Each ACS user
	(Student, faculty, staff) who uses the facility is given a
	copy.  Copies will also be available on-line through several
	sources.

DRAFT | 
DRAFT | 5/4/93	version 3 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | DRAFT!  This is a draft document.  I've not even put it through a
DRAFT | speller. 
DRAFT |
DRAFT | 
DRAFT |                           Appropriate Use of 
DRAFT |                        Winona State University's 
DRAFT |                      Academic Computing Facilites 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | This Statement has these objectives: to clarify what is
DRAFT | appropriate use of academic computing facilities, to state
DRAFT | consequences for violations, to affirm due process for each user
DRAFT | and to state individuals' rights of privacy. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Appropriate Use - the spirit of the statement. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Any use of Academic computing facilities which intentionally
DRAFT | inhibits or disrupts teaching, research, administration or
DRAFT | disciplinary procedures, including the university's public
DRAFT | service functions or other authorized activities on state-owned
DRAFT | or controlled property is prohibited. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Consequences of inappropriate use of ACS facilities 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | The consequences for violation of acceptable use will usually be
DRAFT | the suspension of of access and perhaps probation,
DRAFT | suspension from Winona State University or in the case of a
DRAFT | salaried employee, even termination is possible.  The exact
DRAFT | consequence will vary depending upon the specific violation.  For
DRAFT | example, seriously destructive computing could be considered
DRAFT | destruction of state property and the person subject to civil
DRAFT | action. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Due Process 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | All due process rights as stated in each individual's contract
DRAFT | with the University and the State will be honored.  Students
DRAFT | should refer to the Student Citizenship Standards as published in
DRAFT | the undergraduate catalog. Faculty and staff are protected by
DRAFT | their specific Labor Agreements as published by their union and
DRAFT | excluded management may refer to the Minnesota State University
DRAFT | Board of Regulations for their rights of due process. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Visitors will be considered under faculty or student status,
DRAFT | depending upon their role at WSU.  Typically, a faculty member
DRAFT | who is visiting to do independent research is considered a
DRAFT | student. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Due Process - Statement of Exemption 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | The special exception is intended to protect the property,
DRAFT | health, safety and well being of an accused or another member of
DRAFT | the university.  This exception is extended in some cases to
DRAFT | cause immediate suspension of computer access.  For example, a
DRAFT |   * case in which a user is intentionally causing such
DRAFT |     destruction or is inhibiting work for other users and for which
DRAFT |     an immediate suspension of access is the only remedy because
the
DRAFT |     accused refuses to terminate the process or the accused has
DRAFT |     clearly set it into an autonomous state and is no longer on the
DRAFT |     computer or system;  in other words, in an emergency.  In this
DRAFT |     cases, the account may be suspended immediately, the accused
will
DRAFT |     be notified as soon as possible and has the right to a hearing
DRAFT |     within 72 hours of notification. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Privacy Rights 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Academic computing users have the right to expect that their
DRAFT | materials be private. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | No person, regardless of status (ie; including the system
DRAFT | manager, faculty member or university administrator) may view or
DRAFT | change or remove another users files without the user's
DRAFT | permission, whether the the material exists on a shared computer,
DRAFT | network media or on a student's own media (for example, a
DRAFT | personal floppy disk.) An exception exists for class accounts
DRAFT | which are owned and managed by an instructor or system manager
DRAFT | and which exist strictly for a class project and which exclude
DRAFT | personal electronic mail or personal conferencing: for example a
DRAFT | class statistics account which is reused each semester by
DRAFT | different individuals.  In these cases the administrator of the
DRAFT | account (system manager and or instructor) is allowed complete
DRAFT | access, but persons outside of the class are not. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | EMail is protected as much as possible.  The postmaster (system
DRAFT | administrator) has configured the EMail wherever feasible to
DRAFT | exclude the content of messages that are returned to his/her
DRAFT | attention due to transmission errors or other technical problems.
DRAFT | Like the U.S. Post Office, message contents are to be considered
DRAFT | private when inadvertently viewed. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Privacy - Exceptions. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Files which reside in a user's account, which exist on a user's
DRAFT | own media or the network media are to be considered private
DRAFT | except where: 
DRAFT |  * a runaway program which could be either an
DRAFT |    accidental or an intentional hack, or a virus is in the process
DRAFT |    of causing damage or is inhibiting the work of others. In this
DRAFT |    case it may be necessary for a system manager to inspect the
file
DRAFT |    which may be causing the problem. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Specific Proscriptions: 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | It is important to note that in an educational facility users are
DRAFT | learning as they work and errors occur which may cause system
DRAFT | disruption and that ordinary errors of this type are certainly
DRAFT | beyond the scope of inappropriate use. For example, a poorly
DRAFT | written, runaway C program is not necessarily a violation.  It
DRAFT | may be an accident. The intent of the user is the critical point.
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | Specific examples of inappropriate uses of academic computing
DRAFT | facilities include: 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * gratuitous use of resources such as CPU time or disk space with
DRAFT | the intent of slowing the overall system or obstructing the work
DRAFT | of others. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * copying licensed software from lab micros or from the multiuser
DRAFT | systems for personal use is considered theft. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * intentionally crashing a computer, network or printer or
DRAFT | intentionally making them difficult to access or use. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * erasing or changing another user's files or computer
DRAFT | environment without the users permission. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * causing a user's disk quota to be exhausted and thereby
DRAFT | preventing the individual from working effectively: for example,
DRAFT | 'mail bombing' someone with a deluge of unsolicited messages. 
DRAFT | The content of the messages is irrelevant; it is the intent to
DRAFT | inhibit productivity or damage a user's environment which is of
DRAFT | issue here. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * adding unauthorized software to shared university
DRAFT | microcomputers is not permitted.  The intent of this statement is
DRAFT | largely to maintain a stable environment for public
DRAFT | microcomputers. Adding a game, for example is unacceptable. 
DRAFT | Adding a statistical package is also unacceptable because it also
DRAFT | can disrupt the operation of the micro for others.  However, we
DRAFT | are willing to review and approve exceptions in a timely way;
DRAFT | usually within two working days. Simply contact us. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * intentionally modifying computer interfaces (the look of the
DRAFT | screen.) For example, removing programs or scrambling the icons
DRAFT | on a public Windows, Sun, Macintosh (etc) computer so that it is
DRAFT | difficult to use. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * unauthorized use of university facilities, including buildings,
DRAFT | grounds and equipment.  Academic Computing facilities are only
DRAFT | for the use of WSU student, staff and faculty as well as visitors
DRAFT | who have applied for access. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * use of the facility by an unauthorized individuals.  For
DRAFT | example, you may not permit other students to use your ID card or
DRAFT | your password to use college facilities.  Accounts and IDs are
DRAFT | not to be shared. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * acquiring files when it is clear that the file(s) were intended
DRAFT | to be erased.  Some systems cannot absolutely guarantee that
DRAFT | files are destroyed once deleted and the intentional recovery of
DRAFT | someone else's deleted files is construed to be unauthorized
DRAFT | access and a violation of rights of privacy. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * acquiring privileges or rights in a system which are normally
DRAFT | beyond the scope of the user; for example obtaining root access
DRAFT | to a Unix system or gaining operator rights or discovering
DRAFT | another user's password and using it to gain access to the user's
DRAFT | personal account on a shared system. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * electronic eavesdropping or taping the network or another
DRAFT | computer. Two exceptions exist.  First, where the system manager
DRAFT | must inspect network or system transmissions for purposes of
DRAFT | diagnosis or maintenance and second, for a specific Computer
DRAFT | Science course.  In either case, only protocols and not message
DRAFT | contents (detail) will be observed. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * the use of computing facilities for personal profit making. 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | * the use of WSU computer facilities to hack on other systems at
DRAFT | Winona State or anywhere in the world (the Internet or any
DRAFT | associated network or personal computer) 
DRAFT | 
DRAFT | --- end --- 


